  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nAnother dry and mild October day across the Mid-South as high\npressure dominates the region. Temperatures are once again running\n7 to 10 degrees above normal with relative humidity values in the\nlow to mid 30s. Elevated fire danger will persist today and \ntomorrow afternoon as fuels remain extremely dry and relative \nhumidity hovers around the 30 percent threshold. \n\nA significant shift in the weather pattern will occur this \nweekend as the subtropical jet awakens to our west. The first \nshortwave will arrive late Friday night, but not have much \nmoisture to work with, resulting in only mid to high level clouds.\nSeveral weak perturbations, embedded within the trough, will \npivot through the region on Halloween (Saturday). Mainly light \nshowers will accompany these features Saturday afternoon and \nevening (trick-or-treating time) becoming more widespread \novernight. A few rumbles of thunder may occur overnight into \nSunday morning, mainly south of I-40, as small ribbon of 200 J/kg \nof MUCAPE pivots through. Rainfall totals will range from around \n1/2 an inch west of the Mississippi River with up to an inch to \nthe east. \n\nA brief break in rainfall will occur on Monday and Tuesday \nas we remain in between systems. A more robust and deepening \ntrough will develop over the Plains by Tuesday next week. \nSoutherly flow will advect in Gulf moisture into the lower \nMississippi Valley by Wednesday, resulting in PWATs jumping to \naround 1.5 inches or near the 99th percentile. The trough is \nforecast to evolve into a cutoff low and deliver multiple waves of\nrainfall to the Mid-South Wednesday through late week. NBM \nprecipitation ranges favor 1 to 2 inches with up to 3 inches \nthrough this time period. Temperatures will remain mild and above \nnormal through the entire period with highs in the mid 70s and \nlows in the 50s and 60s each day.\n\nAC3",
